# Vulnerabilities waived for the container image scan in .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# **Empty is the goal.** It went back to empty on 2026-08-17 (#492), on 2026-09-05 (#770), on
# 2026-09-15 (#1273), on 2026-10-17 (#3071) and on 2026-10-26 (#3703), each time by fixing the
# image rather than by amending the waiver.
#
# The gate blocks a release on CRITICAL and HIGH findings **that have a fix available**; unfixed ones
# are already excluded by `--ignore-unfixed` and do not belong here. So an entry in this file means
# something narrower and more deliberate: *a fix exists and we are shipping without it anyway*.
#
# Every entry needs a reason and a date on the line above it, and the reason has to say why the fix
# cannot be taken rather than that it is inconvenient — a waiver with no expiry is a gate that has
# been quietly switched off. The usual right answer instead is to bump the base image digest in the
# Dockerfile, which is one line and takes effect on the next build.
#
# Format: one CVE id per line, `#` comments. Trivy reads this file from the repository root.
#
# Example of the shape an entry must take:
#
#   # 2026-08-12 — CVE-2026-00000 in the JRE base layer. Fixed in temurin 25.0.4, which regresses
#   # <thing>; revisit when 25.0.5 ships. Not reachable from our code path: <why>.
#   CVE-2026-00000
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE LAST EIGHT ENTRIES TAUGHT, KEPT BECAUSE IT WILL HAPPEN AGAIN
#
# Between 2026-08-14 and 2026-08-17 this file held two, then eight, waivers — all for the Go
# standard library compiled into `/usr/bin/pebble`, a service manager carried in Temurin's
# Ubuntu-based JRE and never executed here. Each batch was individually justified: the finding was
# genuinely unreachable, the base-image bump was genuinely tried first, the reason and the date were
# genuinely written down. The policy above was followed exactly, every time.
#
# It was still the wrong outcome. pebble's Go toolchain is frozen until Temurin rebuilds, so the
# list grew on the Go release cadence rather than on anything this project did — a subscription to a
# CVE feed rather than a waiver. #492 changed the base image instead (ADR-017) and all eight
# entries were deleted in the same change.
#
# **So: if a second entry ever appears here for the same root cause as the first, that is the
# signal.** Not the tenth, not when it gets annoying. Two entries sharing a cause means the cause is
# structural, and the question stops being "is this one reachable?" and becomes "why are we still
# shipping the thing that generates them?". Re-making a true argument on a schedule someone else
# sets is how a gate stops being read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 2026-10-26 — back to empty for the fifth time, and by the mechanism every entry is required to
# carry. The CVE-2026-111943 waiver (Go `crypto/x509` in the imgproxy sidecar, added 2026-10-21,
# #3703) named its own deletion condition — "an imgproxy release built with Go 1.27.3 or later" —
# and v4.0.20 is one, five days later and three weeks before the calendar date it also carried. A
# digest bump deleted it, as #1273's entry went in September and #3071's three went in October.
# **A waiver written to expire is a waiver that expires**; that is why the reason has to say what
# would end it.
#
# **The bump is verified by scanning the candidate, never by reading its release notes.** v4.0.19
# builds imgproxy itself with Go 1.27.3 and would have satisfied the condition as written, but it
# moved the base to ubuntu:26.04 and brought in `usr/bin/pebble` at Go 1.27.1 — carrying
# CVE-2026-78667, CVE-2026-78669 and CVE-2026-97031, the three findings #3071 had just removed, in
# the very binary whose eight waivers taught the lesson above. v4.0.20 ships without it. A release
# that fixes the finding you are chasing can arrive with a second copy of one you already closed.
#
# **imgproxy has now been the cause four times** — #1273 (grpc-go), #3071 and #3703 (the Go
# stdlib), and go-jose below. #4188 answered the question the note above names, and ADR-020
# § When to revisit records the answer: waive each episode and wait, because each earlier one ended
# with an upstream release within weeks. **The revisit date is the expiry.** A waiver that reaches
# it with no imgproxy release carrying the fix triggers our own build of imgproxy on a current Go
# toolchain, as a new issue. ADR-020's option B (Thumbnailator) is not the exit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 2026-10-29 — CVE-2026-116587, `github.com/go-jose/go-jose/v4` 4.1.4, in the imgproxy sidecar
# (#4188). Fixed in 4.1.6. The fix cannot be taken: v4.0.20 is the newest imgproxy release and the
# chart already pins it. The advisory is a DoS when go-jose parses a JWK with an RSA key.
# Reachability: imgproxy binds to localhost in the importer pod and only the importer calls it
# (THREAT_MODEL.md B10). Its one TLS peer is Hetzner Object Storage, and nothing we configure gives
# it a JWK. That is a reading of our configuration, not of imgproxy's code.
# Delete when an imgproxy release carries go-jose 4.1.6 or later, by bumping the chart digest.
# Revisit on 2026-11-26. Waived by the #4188 decision (ADR-020 § When to revisit): if no such
# release exists by then, this waiver expires and our own imgproxy build starts as a new issue.
CVE-2026-116587
